# Named roots: a corpus moves by configuration alone [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]

- status: draft
- x-created: 2026-11-02
- scope: A corpus can move to another directory, drive or machine by changing configuration, with no edit to its content and no symlink left behind. One table in the registry names each root, its current location and the spellings it was known by. That table drives where corpora are served from, where a path written in content is now, and where write history lives.
- boundary: The engine reads the table and answers from it. It never moves a file, never edits a corpus to follow a move, and never makes a link. Everything the engine does not serve (scripts, service definitions, virtual environments, git worktrees, tracked links, agent definitions) is the deployment's own migration and is out of reach of this spec. So the engine alone delivers no symlinks only for what it serves: a deployment reaches a hard cutover with no symlinks when it has also migrated those other things. A named root may name any location, corpus or not, so a path written in content that points at a moved script or data folder still resolves; the script itself still has to be moved and fixed by its owner.
- not-this: Not a migration tool. Not a rewrite of stored paths. Not a search feature: a query for the new spelling of a path does not match a node that holds the old spelling. Not multi-writer: one machine writes a root at a time.
- rationale: Maintainers' request of 2026-11-02: a corpus should be movable to any machine and any directory given sufficient configuration, so that it starts and points at valid directories, with a hard cutover and no symlinks; needing symlinks or a rewrite of content raises a design question about how path information has been written to corpora. Measured on one deployment that day: a move by editing registry roots keeps reads working and silently detaches write history, because the ledger file is named by a hash of the real path; and about a fifth of the paths written in its corpora resolved only through a symlink.
- x-flag: LTS_PATH_LOOKUP (environment), default ON whenever the table holds at least one was spelling; set to off to disable. It governs only the reply legend of [MECH-NR-LEGEND]. A registry with no named_roots key, or a table with no was spelling, gives replies byte-identical to the engine without this spec, so nobody pays for or sees it before a move. That byte-identical promise covers the legend only: the absence wording and new_chain of [MECH-NR-LEDGER] apply under every registry. The table, roots, resolver and ledger rules are not flagged.
- x-not-built: Everything in this file. Measured so far: the current failures and a simulation of table size. See [ACC-NR].
- x-reach: Any machine means: a POSIX file system, with a registry that holds the table. A machine under a different user needs the old home spelled in was (GRAM-NR-TABLE); the engine does not guess it. Windows paths are not covered. Parts of the engine import POSIX-only modules today.
- x-authority: Requirement: the maintainers' request above and their ruling in x-open. Everything else here is the design of the project's architects and has no maintainer ruling behind it yet: the table, the resolver and the form of the legend, the writer refusals, where history lives ([MECH-NR-LEDGER]), the state-content property ([PROP-NR-STATE-CONTENT]) and every number in [ACC-NR] (the latency limits, 10 wired lines, about 50 entries). Rewriting content stays a fallback for the maintainers to decide on measured evidence.
- x-open: Closed 2026-11-02: selected by the maintainers from options the architects wrote: both. The registry points corpora at their new folders, and every engine reply tells the reader where a moved path now lives, on by default after a move, with no stored text changed.
- x-not-delivered: History across machines is not delivered, and is parked, not owed (maintainers' ruling of 2026-11-04: for now, no corpus's drive is written from more than one machine). Today a move works on one machine, including onto an external drive, and write history stays in that machine's state folder. Where this file describes a state location, init-state, adopt or history reaching another machine, it describes that parked design, which is not built. See [PLAN-NR-SLICES] x-s2b-parked. A deployment that backs up a corpus must back up the machine's state folder as well, or a restored corpus comes back with no history.

## Invariant: a move changes no corpus byte [INV-NR-CONTENT-UNTOUCHED]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-rule: No code in this spec writes to a corpus file. After a move every node id, content hash and edge is what it was. Replies return stored values byte-identical; a lookup sits beside them, never inside them. This is the design's bar, stricter than the request's words: a one-time owner-run rewrite remains possible as a maintainers' fallback, and is not engine behaviour.
- rationale: A dated record must stay as written, and a mechanism that never edits content cannot corrupt it. It also means no one has to classify which written paths may be changed.

## Invariant: one table, read once [INV-NR-ONE-TABLE]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-rule: Locations come from the named_roots table in the registry file, read once at server boot and covered by the registry hash in registry_choice. Roots, profiles, the resolver, the legend and the ledger all read the same parsed table. Moving a root is one edit, applied as [MECH-NR-ROLLOUT] x-restart requires.
- rationale: Two sources of location is how a move becomes two edits and a drift. The registry is already read once at boot; this keeps that rule.

## Invariant: write history follows the data [INV-NR-HISTORY-FOLLOWS]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-rule: After a move on the same machine, within the table's knowledge, lts_history for a node returns every record it returned before, and the next write continues that history; a copy-then-cut move joins its history when the old copy is gone, with one reported break (x-write in [MECH-NR-LEDGER]). A root that declares a state location takes its history to any machine or mount point where that location is reachable and holds its marker; when it is not, reads serve and writes are refused. A root that declares none leaves its history in that machine's state folder: on another machine the corpus serves and history is declared absent ([INV-NR-ABSENCE-DECLARED]). So history reaches another machine only for roots with a state location, and that is the deployment's choice per root.
- rationale: History that silently detaches or forks on a move makes the reply false and the undo evidence unreachable.
- x-not-delivered: Only the same-machine half of x-rule holds today. The part about a root with a state location is parked: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS] x-not-delivered. A table that declares a state refuses writes under that root, since nothing can begin one.

## Invariant: a write lands where its caller named [INV-NR-NO-SILENT-REDIRECT]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-rule: A typed writer never redirects a path through the table. A path spelled in the old location is refused with the current location in the refusal. Addressing a file by corpus and source_file is the location-free way.
- rationale: A write must land where its caller believes it landed; a read may be helped by a lookup, a write may not.

## Invariant: what the engine cannot find it says it cannot find [INV-NR-ABSENCE-DECLARED]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-rule: No ledger in this state location is reported as exactly that, never as the file having no ledger. A write that begins a new chain says so on its receipt. A half-finished move (a corpus whose every root is missing, a registered profile at a missing path) is declared in lts_list_corpora.
- rationale: Measured today: on a machine with no state folder the reply says the file has NO ledger, the next write starts seq 1 with no warning, and the first machine then reads as not reconciled: a silent fork.

## Invariant: the engine names no deployment [INV-NR-NO-DEPLOYMENT-NAMES]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-rule: Root names, paths and spellings exist only in a deployment's registry. Code, tests and specs use invented examples ([NO-DEPLOYMENT-ID-FAMILIES-IN-SHIPPED-FILES] applies).
- rationale: The engine is public-bound.

## Grammar: the named_roots table [GRAM-NR-TABLE]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-shape: A top-level key named_roots in the registry file, beside the corpus entries: {"named_roots": {"<name>": {"path": "<location>", "was": ["<spelling>", ...], "local": ["<spelling>", ...], "state": "<location>", "private": true}}}. path is required; the rest are optional. A key ending _note is free text, as everywhere in the registry.
- x-name: Matches ^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*$ , the same as an anchor tree name.
- x-path: An absolute path, or one starting ~/ , or relative. Relative resolves against the directory holding the registry file, or against FACTLATTICE_CORPUS_ROOT when that is set, which beats the directory as it does for every relative path in a registry. Not ${name}: a root does not refer to another root in v1.
- x-was: Each spelling is an absolute path, or starts ~/ or $HOME/ , and names a location this root was known by, written as a corpus or ledger may hold it, including the real path of any earlier location. A move adds a spelling and removes none, because dropping one strands the history and lookups that depend on it. The one exception: a root moved back to an earlier location takes that spelling out of was, since it is the current path again. An engine run on another machine or user needs the old absolute home spelled here (for example a root named home with path ~ and was /Users/olduser). The engine does not guess user names.
- x-state: Optional: where this root's write history lives, a location written like a root path or as ${name}/relative. Declaring it is a promise that history is there: see x-state-declared in [MECH-NR-LEDGER]. A state location on a different drive from the root makes every write to that root depend on two drives; keeping them on one drive means they are unavailable together.
- x-private: true: this root is never listed by a no-argument lts_resolve_path call. It still resolves when asked about a path under it and in a legend whose reply text already holds that spelling. No reply names a corpus for a root.
- x-reference: A corpus entry's root path and profile may be written ${name} or ${name}/relative. The form is recognised only at the start of the value. Expansion happens once at registry load, so every consumer of a registry entry sees the same absolute paths it sees today. The relative part stays under the root: a value that starts ${ and is not this form, or whose relative part starts with / or holds a .. component or a control character, is refused named-root-unknown with detail bad-reference, naming no part of the value.
- x-refusals: Closed codes. named-root-unknown: an entry refers to a name not in the table; that corpus fails like an unknown key (failed_corpora) and others serve. named-roots-malformed: the table is not of this shape, a name is bad, a spelling is duplicated across roots, a was spelling equals any root's current path, or a local spelling is not in was; every corpus that uses a name fails with this code and corpora that use none keep serving. named-root-state-inside-corpus: a state location lies inside a registered corpus root; the corpora of that root keep serving reads and every write under it is refused before anything is written. state-unavailable: see [MECH-NR-LEDGER]. A location failing both is reported as named-root-state-inside-corpus, which is a configuration fault and does not go away when a drive returns. named-roots-malformed also refuses every write outside the OS temp dir, on every corpus, and named-root-state-unprovable refuses every write under a root that declares a state, and adopt and init-state, while any registry entry has failed on a key or a name or holds a root that cannot be resolved: none of these can prove where history lives, and a failed entry is never consulted. A corpus whose own entry is sound keeps serving reads under both, and under named-roots-malformed its lts_history serves today's key marked history_partial, since old spellings cannot be searched. A registry that cannot be read at all is not refused: the older registry_unavailable rule stands, and a write goes under today's key with its warning, with new_chain if it starts a ledger. That is visible and recoverable only while no state root exists, so the root marker lands before any state root. A registered root or a state on a volume that is absent is not a failed entry: the corpora and writes of other roots go on, and only files under a root whose declared state is unreachable are refused (x-state-declared). Each refusal states what clears it. (Architects' ruling, 2026-11-03.)
- x-old-engine: An engine that predates this spec reads named_roots as a corpus entry with unknown keys and lists it as a failed corpus while the others serve (measured). It reads ${name}/x as a literal missing path, so such a corpus vanishes. Rollback of the engine therefore restores the prior registry too; see [MECH-NR-ROLLOUT].
- x-any-location: A named root may name a location that holds no corpus. It then serves only the resolver and legend.
- x-local: A registry describes one machine. local lists was spellings that on this machine name something unrelated to this root (the home-relative spelling of an old folder, where this machine happens to have its own folder of that name). A local spelling is ignored here by the resolver, the legend, the writers and the ledger lookup, as is any was spelling that resolves to the same path on this machine, so an unrelated folder's records cannot merge into this root's history. History from the old location is found through its old absolute spelling, which names another machine's path and so does not collide. Each local entry must also appear in was, or the table is malformed.

## Mechanism: registry load and root path fixes [MECH-NR-REGISTRY]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-where: factlattice.registry is the one definition: named_roots joins the schema there (REGISTRY-NOTE-KEYS), and the expansion runs inside read_registry_file so that resolve_registry, entries_from and every caller of either get expanded entries with no change of their own. lts_register_corpus takes the same forms through the same function. The public way for anything outside the engine to read a registry is factlattice.registry.resolved_entries(path), which returns corpus entries only, expanded, with the table stripped; python -m factlattice_engine registry resolved --file <path> prints the same as JSON. A reader that parses the file itself meets a top-level key with no roots and unexpanded ${name} paths, and those readers change before a registry adopts the form.
- x-fix-tilde: A root path starting ~ is expanded when building a corpus. Today it is not expanded at build though membership expands it, and the corpus fails to load (measured: registry loaded 0 corpora). A profile path starting ~ is expanded the same way.
- x-fix-relative: A relative root path in a registry file resolves against that file's directory, or against FACTLATTICE_CORPUS_ROOT when that is set. Today it resolves against the process working directory (measured). This is a behaviour change: the build reads the existing tests on relative roots and the example registry's own rule first, and keeps the example registry as it is.
- x-health: [DECLARED-ROOT-HEALTH] extends to: a registered profile at a missing path (root_health reason profile-missing), and a corpus whose every root is missing, which is listed with status no-roots rather than vanishing from lts_list_corpora.
- x-digest: The registry hash stays over file bytes, with $HOME added when any path or spelling starts with ~ .
- x-ephemeral: [LEDGER-NO-EPHEMERAL-ROOTS] binds the table: a root or state location under the OS temp dir is treated exactly as a registered root there is today.
- x-root-set: A move changes a root's expanded path under an unchanged name, and takes a restart ([INV-NR-ONE-TABLE]). Freshness and [ROOT-SET-CHANGE] compare expanded paths, never names. A table edit on disk while a server runs is not read by it and shows only as registry_choice.sha256_now differing from sha256_at_boot, as today; the restarted server builds from the new location.

## Mechanism: resolving a path through the table [MECH-NR-RESOLVE]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-tool: lts_resolve_path(path). With no argument: the whole table (name, path, was, state, minus private roots), legend: on or off, and no corpus names. CLI: python -m factlattice_engine paths resolve <path>. The flag and its default are stated in SPEC.md and in this tool's description.
- x-match: Normalise the leading ~/ , $HOME/ or ${HOME}/ to the current home, then find the longest spelling, among every root's path and was, that equals the input or is a whole-component prefix of it (/data/ab does not match /data/abc). Replace it with the root's current path. Case is not folded. The input is taken whole: the resolver finds no path inside a sentence.
- x-result: {as_written, now, root, matched_spelling, status, exists, crosses_symlink}. status is one of: moved (table matched, target exists); moved-target-missing (table matched, nothing there); unmoved (no entry matched, exists as written); not-found (no entry matched, nothing there); both-exist (table matched, and the as-written path also exists); not-absolute (the input cannot be resolved). both-exist means only that: two locations answer to this spelling. It may be one thing during seeding or two unrelated things on another machine, the resolver cannot tell which, and it names no winner and claims no sameness. crosses_symlink says whether reaching the result passes through a link. The no-argument reply also lists spellings_existing_here: was spellings, not marked local, that exist on this machine, which paths check repeats.
- x-limits: It reports a location, not an identity: exists does not mean the same bytes; a recorded digest still decides that. It uses stat only and opens no file.
- x-privacy: The query log keeps a digest of the path, never the path, as for lts_find path. A reply for a path under a private root names no corpus.
- x-canonical: The table match is lexical on purpose: a written path may no longer exist, so it cannot be realpath-ed, and it does not fold case. A path that spells a root with a declared state in another case, on a volume where both name one folder by identity, is refused state-unavailable: taken as under the root it would share a ledger and not the per-file lock, and taken as under none it would quietly leave the state. Under a root with no state it keeps today's key, as today. A case variant below a root is not caught, by the same reasoning and as today. The state-inside-corpus check is [L8], which follows [GUARDS-CANONICALISE-FIRST], keeps its existing folding and so errs towards refusal on a case-sensitive volume, deliberately. The path_moved refusal and the was check on create depart from that rule, deliberately: they compare the input and its realpath where it exists against spellings that cannot be realpath-ed, because those locations are gone. They use no case folding, so they never refuse a genuinely different path that differs only in case.

## Mechanism: the lookup legend on a reply [MECH-NR-LEGEND]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-shape: By default once the table holds a was spelling ([FL-NAMED-ROOTS] x-flag), every engine reply carrying a moved spelling adds path_lookup: [{written, now, root}] with one entry per table spelling that occurs, at a whole-component boundary, in that reply, and only for spellings whose root has moved away from them. Absent when there are none. It is added at the one point replies leave the tool layer, scanning the whole reply, so nothing depends on knowing which tools return stored text: lts_history records and writer receipts that show a replaced line are covered like lts_node. There is no exempt list. A coverage test runs every tool on a planted node and fails on any reply carrying a moved spelling with no legend, and a new tool is covered by the same test.
- x-why-prefixes: The legend carries prefixes, not rewritten paths. Finding where a path ends in free text is guesswork (spaces, punctuation, placeholders), and a prefix is all a reader needs. It is scanned from the returned text only, so its cost is bounded by the reply.
- x-not-exact: Never enters nodes, digest, count, total_matched, truncated or complete. It makes no stat of any target, so it says nothing about whether one exists. For an entry whose written spelling itself exists on this machine it adds also_exists: true (one stat per occurring spelling, bounded by the table): the reader then knows the written path may mean the local thing, and calls lts_resolve_path.
- x-tripwire: path_lookup is an absent-when-not-triggered section ([ABSENT-SECTION-TRIPWIRE]): the resolver tool being present and the legend state in its no-argument reply tell a caller that absence means nothing to report.
- x-not-rendered: The engine does not put a legend into rendered text such as agent definitions: measured on one deployment, far fewer link-dependent paths occur there than in corpus text, a boot-time block costs every session and goes stale across the very cut it serves. Paths in such text are corrected by their owners at the cut, with at most one fixed line pointing at lts_resolve_path; the failing path is the trigger.

## Mechanism: paths check, a report-only census [MECH-NR-CHECK]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-command: python -m factlattice_engine paths check --corpus <id> [--json]. Finds path-shaped strings in the corpus by one stated regex, runs each through [MECH-NR-RESOLVE], and reports counts by status and by top folder, plus the listing of each with --json. Exit 0 always unless the check itself failed: it changes nothing and gates nothing.
- x-instrument: It states its regex and its limits in its output (a path holding a space stops at the space, so not-found is a ceiling), and it runs one planted known-present and one known-absent path as a control in the same run, or reports itself as failed.
- x-use: Before a move, to size the table and see what crosses a link. After, to compare with the before listing.

## Mechanism: where write history lives [MECH-NR-LEDGER]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-ruling: Decision of the project's architects, 2026-11-02, from four costed options, measured and not yet a maintainer ruling: history lives with the data. Copying the whole state folder carries every other corpus's history; export and import depend on someone remembering; git history lacks the proposer, reason and discarded values.
- x-locate: One function, factlattice.ledger.locate(real_path), is the only way to a ledger path, and every caller of ledger_path, read_lines and store_blob goes through it. Under a root means under that root's current path, the longest where roots nest; a file at a was spelling that still exists is not under the root. Under a root with a state, the ledger is <state>/ledger/<sha256(name:rel)[:32]>.jsonl. Everywhere else the key stays today's, the hash of the real path, so a server on an old registry and one on a new registry compute the same key for an unmoved file. The per-file write lock is keyed on the real path, so they also share it.
- x-legacy: Ledgers from earlier locations are found through the table, with no migration: the machine state folder is searched for each was spelling not marked local (the table works out once at load which spellings resolve to a local one). A ledger under a was spelling counts as this file's history unless a file exists at that spelling on this machine that is not the same file as the current one: equal real paths first, then equal st_dev and st_ino, so a link left at the old location or a case variant on a case-insensitive volume is the same file and counts. A stat that fails with anything but not-found (a drive mid-mount, an I/O error) means unknown: the ledger is left out with a note, and no write starts from that answer until it clears. Inode numbers are synthesised or unstable on some file systems (exFAT, FAT, some network and sync volumes), where this test can mistake a file for another; a deployment plants the case on the real drive first, in [ACC-NR] c21. A different file there is that file's, whether an old copy during seeding or an unrelated folder: its ledger is left out with a note until the file is gone. A ledger under an old path belongs to the one file the resolver's longest match gives ([MECH-NR-RESOLVE] x-match, across every root), so no ledger is claimed by two current files; another file that could reach it by a shorter spelling has it left out with a note. Where spellings nest, that rule decides, and it may be wrong about a folder that really was a subfolder; the note says so. lts_history returns the union in ts order, reports each source in ledger_files, and reports any break in chain_file_breaks. No existing record is changed; a write may append to one of these files (x-write).
- x-write: A root with no state, in this order and with no choosing by recency: if a ledger exists under the current real path, append to it. Otherwise, if exactly one counts under x-legacy, append to it, so history continues in one file. Otherwise start a ledger under the current real path; with several counted, its receipt carries several_ledgers and lts_history reports the break. A file with none gets new_chain. A write continuing the current ledger beside counted ones carries other_ledgers on its receipt: a writer that does not know the table (an older engine, or a registry without the table) has started a second chain, which x-restart's writers-stopped rule exists to prevent, and history shows it as a break. A file created later at an old spelling takes that key as its own, including records the moved file wrote there after the move: the table can't tell a returned copy from a new file, and history shows the mix as a break. After a copy-then-cut move, while the old copy still exists, writes start a ledger under the current path; when the copy is removed, the old ledger counts, nothing has been lost, and lts_history reports one break at the boundary. A rename, or removing the old copy before the first write, leaves no break. An unmoved file therefore has one key and one lock under any registry, and old and new servers and short-lived processes append to one chain while a registry change takes effect. A root with a state: a write goes to the state location. If no ledger is there and one counts under x-legacy (init-state without adopt), the new record continues it: its ledger_seq is the last legacy seq plus one and its prev_sha256 is the last legacy record's hash. A new field named_root (LEDGER-VERB-VOCAB) records name and rel where a root applies; path_real stays the literal real path at write time.
- x-blobs: Whole-file prior content goes to <state>/ledger-blobs beside the ledger. The read side looks there, then in the machine state folder.
- x-adopt: python -m factlattice_engine ledger adopt --root <name> --registry <candidate> [--dry-run], one-way and explicit, run with every writer to that root stopped: see [MECH-NR-ROLLOUT] step 5. It reads the state location from the candidate registry named, creates it with its marker, moves each file's legacy ledgers into it as one located ledger and copies the blobs they reference, then verifies record counts and chains, prints a manifest of counts and exits non-zero on any disagreement. It moves rather than copies so that history cannot fork. It is idempotent. It covers files this root holds at that moment (not those of a root nested inside it), and nothing else. It refuses, moving nothing, when such a file with a ledger sits at a was spelling of any root, since that ledger may hold a moved file's records. A root added inside an adopted root later leaves its files' history in the outer root's state, unread: nothing moves it. ledger adopt --check --root <name> reports any legacy ledger that has reappeared for those files, which is what a server still on the old table would leave. It says what that means: the records are safe and readable with one reported break, new writes go to the state, and no command folds them in; a maintainer decides.
- x-guards: [L8] applies unchanged to the state location: not inside any registered corpus root and not inside the written file's own directory tree; refused loudly (GRAM-NR-TABLE). Directory 0700, files 0600, as today. A deployment keeps the state location out of its version control itself; the engine does not edit an ignore file.
- x-stays-on-the-machine: Write locks (per-file flock, machine-local), the query log and miss log (telemetry), semantic indexes and anchor check records (rebuildable). Because locks are machine-local, two machines writing one root at once is not protected: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS] not-this.
- x-absence: lts_history under a root whose declared state is unavailable: ledger "state-unavailable", history_complete false, with a note that the corpus serves, history exists at a location not reachable now, and writes are refused until it returns. With nothing found otherwise: ledger stays "none" (the public contract), history_complete false, and the note says no ledger was found in state location <machine or root name> or through the table; history may exist in another state folder or under a spelling the table does not hold; unknown, not absent. No path appears in either note. A receipt for a write that starts a chain carries ledger.new_chain: true with the same reason, on every file's first record.
- x-state-declared: A state location is created only by an explicit act: ledger adopt, or ledger init-state --root <name> --registry <candidate>. Each reads the location from the candidate registry and writes a marker file holding the root name, before the registry that declares it is installed. A write under a root with a declared state checks, inside the per-file lock and before anything is written, that the location exists and holds a marker naming this root. If not, the write is REFUSED with code state-unavailable and nothing is written. So the engine never has to tell an unmounted drive from a state that never existed: an absent location, an empty folder left at an unmounted mount point and another root's folder all refuse, and the only way to begin one is the explicit act. Reads keep serving: the corpus is still content. The marker proves whose folder it is, not that it is whole or current. A state restored from a backup older than the corpus passes the check; a file written since then shows reconciled false on lts_history and the out-of-band warning on its next write, and a build shows this with a planted case.
- x-no-state-declared: A root with no state keeps today's machine folder, which is created on first use. There, nothing on the corpus side records that history exists elsewhere, so a write on another machine cannot be told from a first write: it starts a chain and says so with new_chain, and the fork is visible only on that receipt and as reconciled false on the first machine. This is the design's limit, not a guarantee. A root that moves between machines should declare a state; the engine cannot know a root has moved, so nothing enforces this, and declaring one after a move recovers history only if adopt runs on the machine that still holds the ledgers.

## Property: a state folder holds what the corpus no longer shows [PROP-NR-STATE-CONTENT]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-rule: A ledger record keeps every discarded value verbatim, and a blob keeps a whole earlier file, by design (LEDGER-UNDO-EVIDENCE-DURABLE). So a state location holds content that has been removed from the corpus, plus proposers and reasons. Reading the current corpus says nothing about it, and deleting a value from a corpus does not delete it there.
- x-consequence: Whoever inventories, copies, uploads or shares a drive or folder holding a state location must treat it as content of that sensitivity, and must review it as history, not by looking at current corpus files. A folder listing shows only hashed file names, so names and sizes tell a reviewer nothing about it.
- x-order: adopt takes the history of every file under the root. A corpus that belongs elsewhere leaves the root before adopt runs. After adopt, its history is in that root's state, and it goes wherever that root's drive goes. Moving it out later is a deliberate new step that this spec does not provide.
- x-measure-helper: paths check --state <name> (report-only) gives counts: ledgers, records, records with discarded values and their total size, blobs and their bytes. It prints no value.

## Mechanism: typed writers and moved paths [MECH-NR-WRITERS]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-address: All five typed writers accept corpus + source_file instead of path, resolved as lts_find resolves source_file (with area where a file sits under several roots), exclusive with path. The gate and the receipt are unchanged.
- x-refusal: A path that does not exist and resolves through the table with status moved or moved-target-missing is refused with code path_moved and evidence {now, root}. Nothing is written.
- x-create: lts_add_node(create=true) at a path under any was spelling not marked local is refused path_moved even though the path is free, and refused the same way when that spelling exists on this machine, with evidence both-exist. Otherwise a writer would quietly rebuild a tree the deployment has moved away. A deployment where the folder is genuinely unrelated marks the spelling local.
- x-both: A path that exists is written as named, even under a was spelling (status both-exist while a move is seeding), with a warning on the receipt naming the root.

## Mechanism: rollout and rollback [MECH-NR-ROLLOUT]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-order: 1. Engine lands with no registry change: a registry with no named_roots is byte-identical in behaviour. 1b. Every reader that parses the registry itself moves to resolved_entries. 2 and 3 as one edit, with writers stopped as x-restart requires: the registry gains the table, the legend and resolver wake, and corpus entries convert to ${name}, before any link is removed, with every corpus's resolved roots shown byte-equal before and after by registry resolved on the candidate. 4. The deployment removes its links, on its own evidence. 5. A root gets a state location, one root at a time, when its contents are settled: stop every writer to the root (writes are out for the duration), run ledger adopt against the candidate registry, install the registry, restart every server, run adopt --check. A server not stopped would recreate the legacy files adopt moved.
- x-rollback: Before the combined step 2 and 3 edit, nothing has changed. To undo that edit: restore the prior registry. To the old engine: restore the prior registry as well. For roots with no state, every ledger key the old engine would compute is unchanged, except that a moved file's history continues under its old key, which is unchanged too. A root with a state: after adopt, the old engine sees none of that root's history until roll-forward, and after init-state, none of what was written into the state; adopt has no quick back-out, so it comes last, root by root.
- x-restart: A running server reads the registry at boot and keeps it. Boot happens at launch and at a client reconnect, and from the code, not yet run, at the re-exec a server performs when the engine's source changes, so a later land would bring a pending registry edit into every server ([ACC-NR] c19). A short-lived engine process reads the file each time (measured), so it is on a new registry the moment the file changes. So the risk of a registry edit is the mixed period that starts when the file changes, and restarting servers afterwards does not cover it. A deployment therefore applies each edit with writers stopped: every writer stopped before the file changes and kept stopped through every server's restart, each server proving the registry hash it booted from, rollback stated and shown to work first, and any failure of byte-equal roots stopping the change with the prior registry restored. Whether a no-move edit could later go without the stop is a question for evidence from c16 and c17 once built and measured; until then the stop holds. An edit that changes a location, or touches corpora that sessions read at boot, follows the deployment's cutover procedure as well.
- x-links: Step 4 is not the engine's to clear. paths check reads corpus text and sees nothing of a script, service or program that opens a symlinked path from code, and [ACC-NR] c4 proves only that the engine needs no link in a scratch tree. Both are necessary and neither is enough. A deployment needs its own evidence for other readers, for example an inventory of code paths and a reversible trial: rename a link aside for a watch window, with a one-command restore, and see what fails.

## Acceptance: rehome on a scratch copy, then run the suites [ACC-NR]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-setup: A scratch copy of real corpora: one with no paths in content and several ledger files; one with wired paths and a profile; one with a large ledger; one on a shared root. Moves are renames, not copies. A real caller's shape: fresh processes, only the registry variable set, a fake HOME under a different user name, no harness environment. Sibling directories for home, state and corpus, with an ordinary control write in the same setup before any refusal counts.
- x-red-first: Every check is first shown to fail with the mechanism or the table removed, for the reason it names. A check that has not been seen to fail proves nothing.
- x-c1: Every node id and content_hash, and the edge count, equal before and after.
- x-c2: Every .lts file byte-identical by sha256, apart from the probe writes.
- x-c3: lts_history returns the same count after the move, and the next write continues the seq and chain.
- x-c4: No symlink in the scratch tree (find -type l gives 0).
- x-c5: paths check: everything that resolved before resolves after.
- x-c6: A write by corpus + source_file lands with its gate intact. The old spelling is refused path_moved with the current location. A create under a was spelling is refused.
- x-c7: Cold reader, in the default configuration with nobody setting a flag: a subagent with no context follows 10 wired lines of the corpus with wired paths using only engine replies. All 10, or the legend fails. The coverage test of [MECH-NR-LEGEND] passes on every tool.
- x-c8: Latency on the largest available corpus, p50 and p95, for every read tool that can return a large reply and at least lts_find, lts_node, lts_find_in_prose and lts_traverse, in three configurations: no table; flag off; and the default after a move with the table at its largest expected size, since was spellings are permanent and this is what a deployment runs for life. With no table or with the flag off, replies are byte-identical. The default adds at most 5 ms at p95 to the largest replies.
- x-c9: Recall on the maintainers' semantic recall fixture is unchanged, off vs on.
- x-c10: Absent state, two cases, each with no write landing wrongly. Declared: a root declares state and the location is missing, or is an empty folder with no marker, or holds another root's marker: the corpus serves, lts_history says state-unavailable, the write is refused state-unavailable and the corpus file is byte-identical; when the drive returns, history is whole and the next write continues the chain. Undeclared: a root with no state on a fresh machine: the corpus serves, lts_history declares absence in the new wording, the first write succeeds and says new_chain.
- x-c11: Drive remounted: root path and state move together to a new mount point under a fake HOME, table updated. lts_history is complete and the chain continues, with no was entry needed for the state-held ledgers.
- x-c12: Two machines in turn, for a root that declares a state: writes alternate between two configurations of the same drive (different HOME and mount point). One chain, reconciled true throughout, no fork.
- x-c13: adopt, always with --registry naming a candidate: dry-run changes nothing; a real run gives equal record counts and intact chains, the machine state keeps no ledger for those files, a second run does nothing, and a planted chain break makes it exit non-zero having moved nothing. adopt --check is clean straight after. init-state --registry creates the marker and nothing else.
- x-c14: State location latency, on the real drive that will hold it, in its normal load, not in a scratch tree: 1,000 ledger appends through the typed writer path, p95 at most 50 ms and p99 at most 250 ms per append, and no write waiting past the per-file lock timeout (10 s by default). The internal disk baseline by the library call is 0.18 ms p50, 0.26 ms p95; fsync on an external drive is the unknown. These limits are the architects' judgement, not derived. A miss returns the state-location decision to the architects rather than widening the limit.
- x-suites: The engine's full suite, lts_selfcheck, lts_lint strict on the rehomed copies. Each downstream consumer runs its own suite against the rehomed copies.
- x-falsifies: Any of these sends the design back to the maintainers as the design question they asked: an engine-served wired line that cannot be followed without a content edit; c7 failing; a table that needs an entry per file rather than per folder, past about 50 entries; any node hash change; any history lost or forked.
- x-c15: Unrelated folder at an old spelling: a was spelling exists on this machine as something else, holding a file of the same relative name with its own ledger, and the spelling is NOT yet marked local. The resolver says both-exist and names no winner; the legend entry carries also_exists; a create there is refused path_moved with evidence both-exist; a write to the root's file does not touch that ledger and its records do not appear in the root's history. After the spelling is marked local: the resolver says unmoved, the legend omits it, the create works, and history under the old absolute spelling still appears.
- x-c16: Mixed fleet: one server on the old registry and one on the new write the same file in turn, then together under load, for a file with a ledger and for a file with none, each server writing first in turn. One ledger file per corpus file, one chain, no chain_file_breaks, seq strictly increasing, both gates intact.
- x-c17: Switch under a short-lived writer: fresh-process typed writes run in a loop while the registry file is replaced by the candidate. Every write lands or is refused whole; one chain; none is lost; the corpus is byte-consistent with its ledger.
- x-c18: adopt with a server left running on the old table: adopt --check reports the reappeared legacy ledger and exits non-zero.
- x-c19: Re-exec: with a registry edit pending, land a trivial engine source change on a scratch checkout and read each running server's sha256_at_boot. Each is the new hash without a client restart, or x-restart is corrected to say what does happen.
- x-c20: Several ledgers for one file, as a registry-only move has already left on one deployment: history under an old spelling and under the current one. lts_history returns both in ts order and reports the break; the next write goes to the current one, deterministically.
- x-c21: Copy-then-cut: the old copy stays while the registry points at the new one. Writes land under the current path; lts_history notes the old ledger left out. Remove the copy: the history is whole with one reported break. Variants, each with no break: a link left at the old location; a case variant of the spelling on a case-insensitive volume; a root moved back to an earlier location.
- x-c22: Older state: restore a state folder from before a write. The marker passes; lts_history says reconciled false; the next write carries the out-of-band warning.

## Build order [PLAN-NR-SLICES]

- part-of: [FL-NAMED-ROOTS]
- status: draft
- x-order: S1: table, ${name}, resolved_entries and registry resolved, root path fixes, root health. S2a: locate, the ledger search and x-write, the state-unavailable refusal, the absence wording and new_chain; a table that declares a state refuses writes under that root, since nothing can begin one. S2a alone does not deliver history across machines: it serves a move on one machine, including onto an external drive, whose history stays in that machine's state folder, and a move is not finished against [INV-NR-HISTORY-FOLLOWS] at S2a. S2b, parked (x-s2b-parked): init-state, adopt and adopt --check, with the root marker as a precondition for the first adopt and the same-file test planted on the real drive first. (Split at the adopt seam, architects' ruling of 2026-11-03: every finding that could lose history for good ran through adopt.) S3: lts_resolve_path, paths check. S4: the legend at the reply exit with its coverage test, on by default after a move. S5: writers by corpus + source_file and path_moved. Each slice on a branch, with a test shown failing on the prior commit for the finding's reason and a cold independent check. S2 is the write path and carries the most risk.
- x-extension-points: In the same commits, SDLC 7.6 gains: [NAMED-ROOTS-EXPAND-ONCE] (a reader of a registry entry gets expanded paths from the one function and never expands itself); [LEDGER-LOCATE-ONE-FUNCTION] (no caller builds a ledger path); [PATHS-IN-RECORDS] (a new engine record that stores a location stores root name and relative path where a root applies, and a new kind of record never keys on an absolute path alone; the existing per-file ledger key stays as it is for roots with no state, by decision, because it must match across registries during a change); and the [NODE-CHANGE-LEDGER] line is updated for the new key. SPEC.md states the registry key, the ledger location and every new response key with an absence test. lts_resolve_path follows [CLI-CALL-READ-ALLOW-LIST], [CLI-CALL-COVERAGE] and [QUERYLOG-SEMANTIC-COVERAGE], and joins the tool tuples and parity tests. The codes named-root-unknown, named-roots-malformed, named-root-state-inside-corpus, state-unavailable and path_moved are pinned as stable, by a test, as are resolved_entries and registry resolved, which are a library function and a CLI command and not an MCP tool. The trees configuration of the anchor references spec, if it lands, takes tree paths from this table so that a move stays one edit.
- x-not-in-spec: The review of the path classification asked for alongside this design is a separate deliverable, outside this repository. It does not bind this spec: the design needs no classification.
- x-s2b-parked: S2b is parked by the maintainers' ruling of 2026-11-04, which supersedes "still owed" in x-order for now. It comes back if a corpus's drive is to be written from more than one machine. Smallest S2b then, the architects' sizing and not built or measured: init-state and the root marker, without adopt, for a corpus with little or no history, because x-write in [MECH-NR-LEDGER] already continues a legacy chain into the state. Adopt only if existing history must be carried across, since every finding in S2 that could lose history for good ran through adopt. Before any of it: the root marker, with answers for a restored copy and a backup that excludes dotfiles; the same-file test and c14 on the real drive; a re-run of the census. Under parked S2b the acceptance checks c10 (declared case), c11, c12, c13, c18 and c22 do not apply. Unparked base and open findings are in the project's private records.
